WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.840 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A toy maker wants to post a small box without filling an
envelope with air.

00:00:04.840 --> 00:00:08.560 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Could the box travel as a flat shape and become a solid
later?

00:00:08.560 --> 00:00:12.040 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Today we will investigate that surprising connection.

00:00:12.040 --> 00:00:16.072 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A good net must do more than contain the correct number of
faces.

00:00:16.072 --> 00:00:16.552 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Today we are learning to connect a flat net to the faces of
a solid.

00:00:16.552 --> 00:00:27.632 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
We will move from objects or hands-on actions to drawings,
then connect those drawings with precise mathematical
descriptions.

00:00:27.632 --> 00:00:29.032 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Keep paper nearby if you would like to test an idea for
yourself.

00:00:29.032 --> 00:00:34.585 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
You can pause the player whenever you need more time.

00:00:34.585 --> 00:00:38.425 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Our learning goal is to connect a flat net to the faces of a
solid.

00:00:38.425 --> 00:00:41.225 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
We want to explain why a result is correct, as well as
obtain the result.

00:00:41.225 --> 00:00:48.628 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
As you watch, look for the evidence that would help you
convince a partner who has not seen your working.

00:00:48.628 --> 00:00:51.308 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Here are our three success criteria.

00:00:51.308 --> 00:00:53.508 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
I can match faces and edges.

00:00:53.508 --> 00:00:55.828 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
I can predict and test a fold.

00:00:55.828 --> 00:00:58.228 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
I can explain a gap or overlap.

00:00:58.228 --> 00:01:02.108 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
We will check these ideas in two worked examples,
investigate a common mistake and finish with an independent
challenge.

00:01:02.108 --> 00:01:10.835 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A good explanation links what you see in the model with the
mathematical rule being used.

00:01:10.835 --> 00:01:15.915 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Find a clean empty carton with an adult, or imagine opening
one carefully.

00:01:15.915 --> 00:01:20.395 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Separate its glued tab and unfold the faces without tearing
them apart.

00:01:20.395 --> 00:01:20.635 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Put the flat card on the table.

00:01:20.635 --> 00:01:26.568 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The folds show which faces were neighbours before the carton
was opened.

00:01:26.568 --> 00:01:31.368 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A net is a connected flat arrangement of faces that can fold
into a solid.

00:01:31.368 --> 00:01:34.648 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The faces must meet along matching whole edges.

00:01:34.648 --> 00:01:39.528 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
When folded, they must enclose the object without gaps or
overlapping faces.

00:01:39.528 --> 00:01:43.451 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Glue tabs help construction, but tabs are not additional
faces.

00:01:43.451 --> 00:01:49.331 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Our first net has a column of four squares, with an extra
square on either side of the second square.

00:01:49.331 --> 00:01:51.931 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
All six squares have equal side lengths.

00:01:51.931 --> 00:01:54.451 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Follow each shared edge with your finger.

00:01:54.451 --> 00:01:59.106 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Those joins act like hinges as the card changes from flat to
solid.

00:01:59.106 --> 00:02:01.986 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Predict what will happen when the cross folds.

00:02:01.986 --> 00:02:06.906 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Keep the central square still, bring its four neighbours up,
and think about the last square.

00:02:06.906 --> 00:02:11.706 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Will that last square cover the opening, or land on a face
already occupied?

00:02:11.706 --> 00:02:14.213 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Explain a prediction before we test it.

00:02:19.213 --> 00:02:20.773 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The central square becomes the base.

00:02:20.773 --> 00:02:21.413 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The squares touching its four sides become four walls.

00:02:21.413 --> 00:02:28.773 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The remaining square continues over the top to close the
opening.

00:02:28.773 --> 00:02:31.173 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
There are six separate face positions.

00:02:31.173 --> 00:02:35.125 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Each position is filled once, so this cross is a valid cube
net.

00:02:35.125 --> 00:02:38.405 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Opposite faces are across the solid from each other.

00:02:38.405 --> 00:02:40.885 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
They do not share an edge on the cube.

00:02:40.885 --> 00:02:46.685 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Flat neighbours become neighbours after folding, but two
faces far apart on the paper may also meet.

00:02:46.685 --> 00:02:51.360 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Use the folding model instead of deciding from distance on
the page alone.

00:02:51.360 --> 00:02:56.720 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A classmate arranges six squares in a two by three rectangle
and calls it a cube net.

00:02:56.720 --> 00:03:00.600 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The face count is correct, so that is a sensible first
check.

00:03:00.600 --> 00:03:03.600 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
But folding creates conflicting face positions.

00:03:03.600 --> 00:03:07.280 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Some faces would overlap, leaving another position empty.

00:03:07.280 --> 00:03:09.491 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Counting cannot replace testing.

00:03:09.491 --> 00:03:12.291 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Choose a starting face and label it base.

00:03:12.291 --> 00:03:15.571 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Move from one face to the next across a shared edge.

00:03:15.571 --> 00:03:18.651 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Record where each face would point after folding.

00:03:18.651 --> 00:03:25.547 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
If two different faces claim the same position, stop: the
arrangement cannot form the intended cube without overlap.

00:03:25.547 --> 00:03:31.587 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A triangular prism needs two matching triangular ends and
three rectangular side faces.

00:03:31.587 --> 00:03:35.627 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Use two card triangles and three strips to imagine the
construction.

00:03:35.627 --> 00:03:42.537 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Each rectangle must match one side of each triangle, while
the other rectangle dimension gives the length of the prism.

00:03:42.537 --> 00:03:45.177 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Look at the triangular prism net.

00:03:45.177 --> 00:03:50.537 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Imagine the rectangles wrapping around the sides, while the
two triangles close the ends.

00:03:50.537 --> 00:03:53.257 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Which faces must have matching shapes?

00:03:53.257 --> 00:03:55.177 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Which edges need to meet?

00:03:55.177 --> 00:04:00.311 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Keep those questions separate from simply asking how many
pieces are on the table.

00:04:05.311 --> 00:04:09.311 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The two triangles match exactly and face opposite directions
at the ends.

00:04:09.311 --> 00:04:12.711 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The three rectangles form a band around them.

00:04:12.711 --> 00:04:16.271 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Each triangle side meets a rectangle of the same width.

00:04:16.271 --> 00:04:21.883 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Five faces now enclose the prism: two triangles and three
rectangles, with no open end.

00:04:21.883 --> 00:04:26.403 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A square based pyramid has one square base and four
triangular faces.

00:04:26.403 --> 00:04:29.523 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A triangle can attach to each side of the square.

00:04:29.523 --> 00:04:29.683 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
When the triangles fold inward, their tips meet at the apex.

00:04:29.683 --> 00:04:39.713 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The triangle dimensions must fit that pyramid; arbitrary
triangles will not necessarily meet correctly.

00:04:39.713 --> 00:04:47.153 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Both our triangular prism and square based pyramid have five
faces, but their nets have different face shapes and
connections.

00:04:47.153 --> 00:04:50.952 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The prism has two triangular ends and three rectangles.

00:04:50.952 --> 00:04:54.112 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The pyramid has one square and four triangles.

00:04:54.112 --> 00:04:58.286 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Describe those differences rather than relying on the shared
total.

00:04:58.286 --> 00:04:59.006 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Imagine a cube net made from only five equal squares.

00:04:59.006 --> 00:05:05.086 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Can folding alone create the missing face?

00:05:05.086 --> 00:05:09.046 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Decide whether the problem is a gap, an overlap, or both.

00:05:09.046 --> 00:05:15.291 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Tell a partner what evidence you would seek before calling
any unfamiliar arrangement a valid net.

00:05:20.291 --> 00:05:23.731 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Five squares cannot supply all six cube faces.

00:05:23.731 --> 00:05:26.651 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
One face position must remain uncovered.

00:05:26.651 --> 00:05:31.371 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Folding changes the directions of the faces; it does not
create another square.

00:05:31.371 --> 00:05:38.555 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A valid construction needs the correct faces, correct
dimensions, and a connection pattern that closes without
overlap.

00:05:38.555 --> 00:05:40.355 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Return to our learning goal.

00:05:40.355 --> 00:05:43.275 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Connect a flat net to the faces of a solid.

00:05:43.275 --> 00:05:48.235 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Choose one example from today and explain the evidence that
made its answer convincing.

00:05:48.235 --> 00:05:52.915 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Which of our three success criteria feels secure, and which
needs another try?

00:05:52.915 --> 00:05:59.448 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Revisit a model, test your explanation with a partner, and
describe one mistake you can now notice and correct.
